# The consumer's test-runtime dependencies. Regenerate the pinned lock with:
#   pip-compile --output-file=requirements.txt --strip-extras requirements.in
#
# The framework, pinned to an immutable tag in its own repository. Everything
# it needs - pytest, playwright, allure-pytest, pytest-rerunfailures - arrives
# through it and is pinned by the lock below, so this file no longer repeats
# the framework's own dependency floors. `[report]` is what makes the --html in
# pytest.ini work (pytest rejects an option no installed plugin defines, before
# collection), and `[ai]` brings the scenario-catalog validator's PyYAML and
# jsonschema so the catalog self-tests run instead of skipping.
#
# Rolling back is a one-line change of the ref.
#
# One caveat, measured rather than assumed: `pip-compile` copies this VCS
# requirement into the lock *as written*, with the tag - it does not rewrite it
# to the commit the tag resolves to, the way `pip freeze` does. The tag is
# immutable by policy (it is never moved or re-pointed), and the commit it
# resolved to when this lock was generated is recorded here so the pairing
# survives in version control:
#
#   v0.1.0 -> 215065ce1425e45f53db039e7a0d0d306078b560
playwright-pytest-framework[ai,report] @ git+https://github.com/abhay-scaler/playwright-pytest-framework@v0.1.0

# Genuinely this project's own: parallel execution is a choice about how this
# suite runs, so the framework does not declare it, even optionally.
pytest-xdist>=3.0.0

# Lint and type tooling for this repository.
black>=24.0.0
ruff>=0.6.0
mypy>=1.11.0
